{%- comment -%}
  PG Gallery Tweaks (orbs product page): the video first, then the swipeable
  strip of orb photos. Nothing else.

  WHAT PG-LANDING BUILDS. The gallery column renders three things in this order:

      .pgx-hero        one big still, driven by the strip below it
      .pgx-vid         the product clip
      .pgx-thumbwrap   the strip of every character image (media[1...])

  THE HERO IS GONE, by request. It was the five-orbs-together group shot
  (media[0], alt "Crystal PokeOrb - HERO"), which is the right picture on a
  collection card -- it is selling the range -- and the wrong one to open a page
  that is selling a single orb. Earlier passes tried to move the hero off it to
  the first real character image; the ask now is simpler, so the hero is hidden
  outright and the video leads the page. With .pgx-hero out, the DOM order that
  is left IS the requested order: video, then the strip.

  It is HIDDEN, not deleted, and the product's media is untouched. Deleting the
  group shot would strip it from the product card too, which was explicitly to
  be kept, and media deletion cannot be undone. Hiding is also why the strip
  keeps working unchanged: its buttons still set .pgx-hero's src, they simply
  set it on something nobody sees.

  ONE PHOTO PER VIEW ON DESKTOP TOO. pg-gallery-mobile already makes the strip
  one-orb-at-a-time under 900px. Above it, pg-landing lays the strip out two-up,
  which was sized to sit UNDER a big hero as thumbnails. With the hero gone the
  strip is the gallery, so a thumbnail-sized pair is too small to be the only
  picture of the product: desktop gets the same one-per-view sizing, paged by
  the arrows pg-landing already draws on that breakpoint.

  THE SWIPE HINT. pg-gallery-mobile deliberately hides the carousel arrows on
  phones, because they sat on top of the orbs. That fixed the covering but left
  no sign the strip scrolls, so the photos read as a dead row.

  IT SITS ON THE PHOTO, pinned to the top edge of the orb the strip is showing.
  Placement has been the whole problem with this hint and it took two passes:

    - UNDER the strip it measured y=925 on a 390x844 phone. The strip is ~358px
      tall, so the note telling you to swipe was itself below the fold, beneath
      the very photos it described. Nobody scrolls past a row to be told the row
      scrolls.
    - BETWEEN the video and the strip it was on screen, but it read as a caption
      for the VIDEO -- it was a sibling sitting directly under the clip, and
      nothing tied it to the photos below it.

  So it is now absolutely positioned inside .pgx-thumbwrap, centred at the top of
  the first photo. It is unambiguously about the strip, because it is ON the
  strip. It stays pointer-events:none and only overlaps the top ~50px of the
  orb, so nothing is blocked and the subject is not covered.

  IT STAYS. This has been whittled down twice and both passes were still
  removing it too eagerly:

    - It was suppressed for good in localStorage on the first strip scroll, so
      one stray swipe ever and the hint was dead in that browser.
    - Then it was removed on the first `scroll` OR `touchmove` of THIS page
      view. `touchmove` is the problem: a finger that starts on the gallery and
      drags DOWN scrolls the page, not the strip, and that fires touchmove on
      the strip -- so simply scrolling past the photos killed the hint, which is
      exactly the "it goes away when you scroll" being reported.

  So it is not removed at all any more. It is a standing label, like the
  character name at the bottom of the same photo: while the strip has more
  photos than fit, it says so. It is pointer-events:none, sits on the top ~50px
  of the orb, and carries the shopper's position in the strip (3/35) so it also
  answers "how many more" rather than only "there are more".

  THE COUNTER KEEPS UP WITH THE FINGER. It was repainted by a 90ms debounce off
  `scroll`, which is precisely wrong for this: each new scroll event cleared the
  pending timer, so through a continuous swipe the number sat frozen and only
  jumped once, at the end, when the strip settled. paint() runs on every
  animation frame instead. It is kept cheap enough to afford that -- the button
  centres are measured once and cached, so a frame costs 35 subtractions and no
  layout read. label() is the same pass with the names fetch awaited in front of
  it, and still runs on a trailing debounce so a late fetch is not stranded.

  pg-landing rebuilds the gallery after its fetch and throws the node away, so
  every pass re-checks that it is still in the document and re-inserts it.

  Mobile only; desktop keeps its arrows.

  ================================================================
  THE CHARACTER NAME, ON THE PHOTO AND PERMANENT.

  The strip is 35 photos of 35 different characters and nothing on screen said
  which one you were looking at -- the name only appeared once you opened the
  size picker. The name now sits on the photo itself, in the same pill style as
  the swipe hint but anchored to the BOTTOM so the two never collide, and unlike
  the hint it never goes away: it is not a tip to be learned, it is a label.

  WHERE THE NAMES COME FROM. Not from the strip markup -- pg-landing renders
  every thumbnail with alt "Crystal Legends Orb view N", which carries no name.
  The product's MEDIA does carry them, as "Crystal PokeOrb - 13 Arceus", so the
  names are read once from /products/{handle}.js and the prefix trimmed off.

  MATCHED ON FILENAME, NOT ON INDEX. The obvious mapping -- strip button i to
  media[i+1] -- happens to work today but is one media upload away from labelling
  every orb with its neighbour's name. The counts already disagree (37 media, 36
  images, 35 buttons: media[0] is the group shot, and one media entry is the
  video, which is not an image). So each button's own <img> src filename is
  matched against the media list instead, which cannot drift: an unmatched photo
  simply gets no label rather than a wrong one.

  Registered in templates/product.pg-crystal.json rather than a section group:
  the overlay group is at Shopify's hard limit of 25 sections, and this belongs
  to one product page anyway.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- comment -%}
  THE NAMES ARE RENDERED, NOT FETCHED. They used to arrive from
  /products/{handle}.js, which is why the name pill appeared a beat after the
  counter next to it: the counter is pure arithmetic over the DOM and paints on
  the first frame, while the name sat behind a network round trip that costs
  300-800ms on a phone. The same media list is already available to Liquid here,
  so it ships with the page and the first paint has it. The fetch is kept only
  as a fallback for the case where this section renders without a product in
  scope.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- if product and product.media.size > 0 -%}
<script type="application/json" id="pg-gt-names">
[{%- for m in product.media -%}{%- unless forloop.first -%},{%- endunless -%}{"a":{{ m.alt | default: '' | json }},"s":{{ m.preview_image.src | split: '?' | first | split: '/' | last | json }}}{%- endfor -%}]
</script>
{%- endif -%}
<style>
  /* the group shot -- see the note above */
  #pgx .pgx-hero{display:none !important}
  /* the video now leads the column, so it does not need the gap that separated
     it from the hero */
  #pgx .pgx-vid{margin-top:0 !important}

  /* the strip is the gallery now, not a row of thumbnails under one */
  @media (min-width:901px){
    #pgx .pgx-thumbwrap .pgx-thumbs button{flex:0 0 100% !important}
  }

  /* pinned to the top of the photo the strip is showing -- see the note above.
     .pgx-thumbwrap is position:relative on both breakpoints (pg-landing sets it,
     pg-gallery-mobile re-asserts it under 900px), so this anchors to the strip. */
  #pgx .pgx-thumbwrap .pg-gt-swipe{
    position:absolute;
    top:8px;left:50%;transform:translateX(-50%);
    z-index:4;
    margin:0;
    background:rgba(11,7,16,.82);
    border-color:rgba(190,140,235,.55);
    box-shadow:0 6px 18px -6px rgba(0,0,0,.8);
  }
  /* the character name -- see the note above. Bottom-anchored so it can never
     collide with the swipe hint at the top, and pointer-events:none so it is a
     label rather than a control. */
  #pgx .pgx-thumbwrap .pg-gt-name{
    position:absolute;
    bottom:10px;left:50%;transform:translateX(-50%);
    z-index:4;
    max-width:calc(100% - 24px);
    padding:6px 14px;
    border-radius:999px;
    background:rgba(11,7,16,.82);
    border:1px solid rgba(190,140,235,.55);
    box-shadow:0 6px 18px -6px rgba(0,0,0,.8);
    color:#F1EDF7;
    font-size:12.5px;font-weight:800;
    letter-spacing:.06em;text-transform:uppercase;
    white-space:nowrap;overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis;
    text-align:center;
    pointer-events:none;
  }
  @media (max-width:430px){
    #pgx .pgx-thumbwrap .pg-gt-name{font-size:11.5px;padding:5px 12px}
  }

  #pgx .pg-gt-swipe{
    display:none;
    align-items:center;justify-content:center;gap:10px;
    margin:0 auto;
    padding:11px 20px;
    width:max-content;max-width:100%;
    border-radius:999px;
    background:rgba(139,68,190,.18);
    border:1px solid rgba(190,140,235,.50);
    color:#EEE1FB;
    font-size:15px;font-weight:800;
    letter-spacing:.10em;text-transform:uppercase;
    white-space:nowrap;
    pointer-events:none;
  }
  #pgx .pg-gt-swipe span{display:inline-block;font-size:23px;line-height:.7;color:#F6ECFF;animation:pgGtNudge 1.5s ease-in-out infinite}
  /* the counter repaints on every scroll frame now, so it is fixed-width:
     tabular figures plus a min-width sized for the widest reading ("35/35")
     stop the centred pill shimmying as the digits change under the finger. */
  #pgx .pg-gt-swipe b.pg-gt-n{
    display:inline-block;
    min-width:4.8ch;text-align:center;
    font-weight:800;color:#FFFFFF;letter-spacing:.04em;
    font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums;
  }
  #pgx .pg-gt-swipe .pg-gt-l{animation-direction:reverse}
  @keyframes pgGtNudge{
    0%,100%{transform:translateX(0);opacity:.55}
    50%    {transform:translateX(4px);opacity:1}
  }
  @media (max-width:900px){
    #pgx .pg-gt-swipe.pg-gt-on{display:flex}
  }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion:reduce){
    #pgx .pg-gt-swipe span{animation:none}
  }
</style>
<script>
(function(){
  if (window.pgGalTweaks) return; window.pgGalTweaks = 1;

  var el = null;

  /* ---- the character name on the photo ---- */
  var NAMES = null;                       /* filename fragment -> character name */

  /* "Crystal PokeOrb - 13 Arceus" -> "13 Arceus"; anything without a separator
     is left whole rather than guessed at */
  function trimName(alt){
    if (!alt || /hero/i.test(alt)) return '';
    return alt.split(/\s[-–—]\s/).pop().trim();
  }

  /* the names the page shipped with -- no network, so the first paint has them */
  function readInline(){
    if (NAMES) return true;
    var tag = document.getElementById('pg-gt-names');
    if (!tag) return false;
    var arr;
    try { arr = JSON.parse(tag.textContent); } catch (e) { return false; }
    if (!arr || !arr.length) return false;
    NAMES = {};
    for (var i = 0; i < arr.length; i++){
      var name = trimName(arr[i] && arr[i].a);
      if (name && arr[i].s) NAMES[arr[i].s] = name;
    }
    return true;
  }

  /* one fetch, however many callers. label() runs off a 120ms scroll debounce
     AND five boot timeouts, and NAMES stays null until the response lands -- so
     on the fallback path every one of those callers used to start its own
     request for the same file. The in-flight promise is shared instead. */
  var NAMESP = null;

  function loadNames(){
    if (NAMES) return Promise.resolve(NAMES);
    if (readInline()) return Promise.resolve(NAMES);
    if (NAMESP) return NAMESP;
    var h = (location.pathname.match(/\/products\/([^\/?#]+)/) || [])[1];
    if (!h) { NAMES = {}; return Promise.resolve(NAMES); }
    return (NAMESP = fetch('/products/' + h + '.js', {headers: {'Accept': 'application/json'}})
      .then(function(r){ return r.ok ? r.json() : null; })
      .then(function(j){
        NAMES = {};
        (j && j.media || []).forEach(function(m){
          var src = m.src || '';
          var name = trimName(m.alt);
          if (!name) return;
          var file = (src.split('/').pop() || '').split('?')[0];
          if (file) NAMES[file] = name;
        });
        return NAMES;
      })
      .catch(function(){ NAMES = {}; return NAMES; }));
  }

  function nameFor(img){
    if (!NAMES || !img) return '';
    var file = ((img.getAttribute('src') || '').split('/').pop() || '').split('?')[0];
    if (NAMES[file]) return NAMES[file];
    /* the strip requests a resized copy, so the stored name is looked up by the
       filename STEM as well -- still a filename match, never an index guess */
    var stem = file.replace(/\.(png|jpg|jpeg|webp|gif)$/i, '');
    for (var k in NAMES){ if (k.indexOf(stem) === 0 || stem.indexOf(k.replace(/\.[^.]+$/, '')) === 0) return NAMES[k]; }
    return '';
  }

  /* ---- which photo is in view ----
     The counter used to be repainted by a 90ms debounce hung off `scroll`, and
     a debounce is the one thing it must not be: during a continuous swipe every
     new scroll event cleared the pending timer, so the number did not move AT
     ALL until the finger lifted and the strip settled. It repaints on every
     animation frame instead, so it counts up under the finger.

     That per-frame budget is why the button centres are cached. Reading
     offsetLeft off 35 buttons every frame would force a synchronous reflow each
     time, because the frame before it wrote to the counter's textContent. The
     cache is keyed on the three things that can move those centres -- a
     different strip node, a different number of photos, a different strip width
     -- so pg-landing swapping the strip out invalidates it on identity alone. */
  var MIDS = null;

  function midsOf(strip, btns){
    if (MIDS && MIDS.strip === strip && MIDS.n === btns.length && MIDS.w === strip.clientWidth) return MIDS.v;
    var v = [];
    for (var i = 0; i < btns.length; i++) v.push(btns[i].offsetLeft + btns[i].offsetWidth / 2);
    MIDS = {strip: strip, n: btns.length, w: strip.clientWidth, v: v};
    return v;
  }

  /* synchronous and allocation-light -- safe to call once per frame */
  function paint(){
    var wrap = document.querySelector('#pgx .pgx-thumbwrap');
    if (!wrap) return;
    var strip = wrap.querySelector('.pgx-thumbs');
    if (!strip) return;
    var btns = strip.querySelectorAll('button');
    if (!btns.length) return;

    /* the photo whose centre is nearest the strip's */
    var mids = midsOf(strip, btns);
    var mid = strip.scrollLeft + strip.clientWidth / 2;
    var best = -1, bestD = Infinity;
    for (var i = 0; i < mids.length; i++){
      var d = Math.abs(mids[i] - mid);
      if (d < bestD){ bestD = d; best = i; }
    }
    if (best < 0) return;

    /* the swipe pill's counter */
    var cnt = wrap.querySelector('.pg-gt-swipe .pg-gt-n');
    if (cnt){
      var pos = (best + 1) + '/' + btns.length;
      if (cnt.textContent !== pos) cnt.textContent = pos;
    }

    /* the name needs the media fetch; until that lands label() drives it */
    if (!NAMES) return;
    var text = nameFor(btns[best].querySelector('img'));
    var tag = wrap.querySelector('.pg-gt-name');
    if (!text){ if (tag && tag.parentNode) tag.parentNode.removeChild(tag); return; }
    if (!tag){
      tag = document.createElement('div');
      tag.className = 'pg-gt-name';
      tag.setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'true');
      wrap.appendChild(tag);
    }
    if (tag.textContent !== text) tag.textContent = text;
  }

  /* the same pass, but waiting on the names fetch first */
  function label(){ loadNames().then(paint); }

  function hint(){
    if (el && el.isConnected) return;          /* still on screen, nothing to do */
    var wrap = document.querySelector('#pgx .pgx-thumbwrap');
    if (!wrap) return;
    var strip = wrap.querySelector('.pgx-thumbs');
    if (!strip) return;

    if (strip.scrollWidth <= strip.clientWidth + 4) return;   /* nothing to swipe to */

    el = document.createElement('div');
    el.className = 'pg-gt-swipe pg-gt-on';
    el.setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'true');
    el.innerHTML = '<span class="pg-gt-l">&lsaquo;</span> Swipe &middot; <b class="pg-gt-n"></b> <span>&rsaquo;</span>';
    /* ON the strip, not above it -- see the note above */
    wrap.appendChild(el);
  }

  function boot(){
    if (!document.getElementById('pgx')) return;
    readInline();      /* so the very first paint already knows the names */
    hint();
    paint();
    label();
    /* `scroll` does not bubble, but it DOES capture, so listening on the
       document catches the strip's own scrolling and -- unlike a listener bound
       to the strip node, which is what this used to be -- survives pg-landing
       throwing the gallery away and rebuilding it. */
    var raf = 0;
    document.addEventListener('scroll', function(e){
      var t = e.target;
      if (!t || !t.classList || !t.classList.contains('pgx-thumbs')) return;
      if (!raf) raf = requestAnimationFrame(function(){ raf = 0; paint(); });
      /* one trailing pass so the NAME still settles if the fetch landed late */
      clearTimeout(label._t);
      label._t = setTimeout(label, 120);
    }, {capture: true, passive: true});

    var root = document.getElementById('pgx');
    if (root && window.MutationObserver){
      var queued = false;
      /* pg-landing rebuilds the gallery after its fetch */
      new MutationObserver(function(){
        if (queued) return;
        queued = true;
        MIDS = null;
        setTimeout(function(){ queued = false; hint(); label(); }, 80);
      }).observe(root, {childList: true, subtree: true});
    }
    window.addEventListener('resize', function(){ MIDS = null; label(); }, {passive: true});
    [300, 900, 2000, 4000, 7000].forEach(function(t){ setTimeout(hint, t); setTimeout(label, t); });
  }

  if (document.readyState === 'loading') document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', boot);
  else boot();
})();
</script>
{% schema %}
{"name": "PG Gallery Tweaks", "settings": [], "presets": [{"name": "PG Gallery Tweaks"}]}
{% endschema %}
